{"step_index":290,"source":"MODEL","type":"PLANNER_RESPONSE","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-09-10T09:45:15Z","content":"The network diagnostic test confirms the exact state of the whole chain:\n\n---\n\n### The Verdict\n\n1. **The Application & Proxy Chain**: <span style=\"color:green\">**100% OPERATIONAL**</span>\n   When connecting from backend `172.30.198.49` directly to the Reverse Proxy (`172.30.198.33:9443`), it connects and returns the Centreon Map application response:\n   ```text\n   HTTP/2 401 Unauthorized\n   Content-Type: application/json;charset=ISO-8859-1\n   {\"code\":401,\"message\":\"Full authentication is required to access this resource\"}\n   ```\n   * The Reverse Proxy (`172.30.198.33`) properly redirects and proxies to Centreon Map (`172.30.198.50:9443`).\n\n2. **The Public VIP Route (`109.62.64.18:9443`)**: <span style=\"color:red\">**DROPPED BY FIREWALL (Hairpin NAT)**</span>\n   During a synchronized packet capture on `172.30.198.33` while `172.30.198.49` sent requests to `https://109.62.64.18:9443/`:\n   ```text\n   === TCPDUMP ON REVERSE PROXY (172.30.198.33) ===\n   0 packets captured\n   ```\n   **Zero packets reached `172.30.198.33`**. The firewall (`172.30.198.54`) dropped the packet at the perimeter.\n\n---\n\n### Why the Firewall Drops It: Missing \"Hairpin NAT\"\n\nIn enterprise firewalls (FortiGate, Palo Alto, Check Point), an inbound DNAT/VIP rule on the WAN interface **only matches traffic originating from the Internet**.\n\nWhen an internal server (`172.30.198.49`) tries to reach its own public VIP (`109.62.64.18`):\n* The firewall ignores the WAN DNAT rule for internal traffic and silently drops it unless **Hairpin NAT (U-turn NAT)** is explicitly configured.\n\n---\n\n### How to Fix It (2 Options)\n\n#### Option 1: Split-DNS or `/etc/hosts` on Backend (Instant Fix — Recommended)\nOn Centreon Web (`172.30.198.49`), map the hostname directly to the reverse proxy internal IP:\n```bash\n# Add to /etc/hosts on 172.30.198.49\n172.30.198.33 map.extranetclient.outremer-telecom.fr\n```\n* **Result**: `172.30.198.49` communicates directly with `172.30.198.33:9443` over the LAN with zero firewall overhead, optimal throughput, and zero external dependency.\n\n#### Option 2: Firewall Hairpin NAT Policy\nOn your firewall (`172.30.198.54`):\n1. Apply the DNAT / VIP rule to the **internal interface** (LAN/DMZ) as well as the WAN.\n2. Enable **SNAT (Source NAT / Masquerade)** on that rule.\n   *(SNAT is mandatory here to prevent asymmetric routing, since `.49` and `.33` are in the same `172.30.198.0/24` subnet).*","thinking":"**Confirming direct service accessibility**\n\nDirectly accessing the reverse proxy from the backend using a `curl` request yielded an immediate `401 Unauthorized` response. This suggests the reverse proxy is operational and accessible from the backend. The entire application chain appears to be functioning correctly based on this test.\n\n\n"}
