{"step_index":303,"source":"MODEL","type":"PLANNER_RESPONSE","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-09-10T09:52:37Z","content":"Here is the exact packet-by-packet breakdown of what happens when you run `curl https://109.62.64.18:443` on the backend (`172.30.198.49`):\n\n---\n\n### Step 1: Routing Decision on the Backend\n* The Linux kernel on `172.30.198.49` checks its routing table:\n  ```text\n  109.62.64.18 via 172.30.198.54 dev ens192\n  ```\n* Because `109.62.64.18` is a public IP outside the local subnet, the backend wraps a TCP SYN packet and sends it to its default gateway: **the firewall at `172.30.198.54`**:\n  * **Source IP**: `172.30.198.49`\n  * **Destination IP**: `109.62.64.18`\n  * **Destination Port**: `443`\n  * **Flag**: `[SYN]`\n\n---\n\n### Step 2: The Firewall Inspection\nThe packet arrives on the firewall's internal interface (`172.30.198.54`). The firewall evaluates two lookup tables:\n\n1. **NAT / VIP Table**:\n   * *Is there a DNAT rule for `109.62.64.18` on port **`443`**?* \n     $\\rightarrow$ **No.** The firewall only has a DNAT rule for port **`9443`** on that IP. (Port 443 belongs to `109.62.64.27`).\n   * *Is Hairpin NAT enabled on the internal interface?* \n     $\\rightarrow$ **No.**\n2. **Security Policy**:\n   * With no NAT translation matching port `443`, the firewall finds no authorized policy allowing internal traffic to terminate on its own public VIP on port 443.\n\n---\n\n### Step 3: The Packet is Dropped (Black Hole)\n* The firewall silently discards (drops) the TCP SYN packet.\n* **The reverse proxy (`172.30.198.33`) never receives a single packet** — it is completely unaware of the attempt.\n\n---\n\n### Step 4: The Result in Your Terminal\n* Because the SYN packet was swallowed by the firewall, the backend waits for a `SYN-ACK` that never comes.\n* The backend TCP stack sends retransmissions until `curl` times out:\n  ```text\n  * Trying 109.62.64.18...\n  * TCP_NODELAY set\n  * Connection timed out after 3001 milliseconds\n  * Closing connection 0\n  curl: (28) Failed to connect to 109.62.64.18 port 443: Connection timed out\n  ```\n\n---\n\n### In Summary\nThe packet dies at the firewall gateway (`172.30.198.54`) because:\n1. **Port 443 has no DNAT rule** on `109.62.64.18`.\n2. **No Hairpin NAT policy** exists to loop internal traffic back inside.","thinking":"**Tracing the `curl` Execution**\n\nThe backend system determines the route for the destination IP address, likely using its default gateway. Next, it constructs an IP packet with the destination IP `109.62.64.18` and source IP `172.30.198.49`, then forwards this packet. This IP packet is encapsulated in a data-link frame, and transmitted based on the ARP cache (or ARP resolution if needed).\n\nThe `curl` command specifies HTTPS, so the transport layer will establish a TCP connection to port 443 on the target IP. This process includes a three-way handshake before transferring the HTTP request over the established, secure connection.\n\n\n"}
