{"step_index":305,"source":"MODEL","type":"PLANNER_RESPONSE","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-09-10T09:54:09Z","content":"Here is the precise, packet-level breakdown of what happens when you run `curl https://109.62.64.18:9443` on the backend (`172.30.198.49`), contrasting it with port 443:\n\n---\n\n### Step 1: Egress from the Backend\n1. The backend (`172.30.198.49`) prepares a TCP SYN packet:\n   * **Source**: `172.30.198.49:ephemeral_port`\n   * **Destination**: `109.62.64.18:9443`\n   * **TCP Flag**: `[SYN]`\n2. Because `109.62.64.18` is a public IP, the backend sends the Ethernet frame to its default gateway: the firewall (`172.30.198.54`).\n\n---\n\n### Step 2: The Firewall Ingress & Evaluation\nThe packet enters the firewall on its **Internal (LAN/DMZ) interface**. This is where standard DNAT breaks:\n\n```\n[Backend 172.30.198.49] \n         │ \n         ▼ (SYN to 109.62.64.18:9443)\n[Firewall Internal Interface: 172.30.198.54]\n         │\n         ├── Check 1: Does a DNAT rule exist for 109.62.64.18:9443?\n         │            --> YES.\n         │\n         ├── Check 2: What is the \"Incoming Interface\" on that DNAT rule?\n         │            --> In standard configs, it is set to \"WAN\" (Internet).\n         │            --> The packet arrived on \"LAN\", so the rule DOES NOT MATCH.\n         │\n         └── Check 3: Is there a Hairpin NAT (U-turn) policy for LAN -> VIP?\n                      --> NO.\n                      --> Packet is silently DROPPED.\n```\n\nBecause the DNAT rule is bound to the WAN interface, the firewall treats the packet as traffic directed to its own public IP from an internal zone without an authorized policy, and **drops it immediately**.\n\n*(This is proven by the `tcpdump` we ran on the reverse proxy `172.30.198.33`, which showed **`0 packets received`**).*\n\n---\n\n### Step 3: What if the Firewall DID translate it? (The Asymmetric Routing Trap)\n\nEven if the firewall is set to match the VIP on \"Any Interface\", without a proper **Hairpin SNAT**, the connection still fails due to **Asymmetric Routing**:\n\n```\n1. Backend sends SYN:      Src: 172.30.198.49  ──> Dst: 109.62.64.18 (Firewall)\n2. Firewall DNATs Dst:     Src: 172.30.198.49  ──> Dst: 172.30.198.33 (Reverse Proxy)\n3. Proxy sends SYN-ACK:    Src: 172.30.198.33  ──> Dst: 172.30.198.49 (DIRECT VIA LOCAL SWITCH!)\n```\n\n* Because `.49` and `.33` are on the **exact same subnet** (`172.30.198.0/24`), the reverse proxy does not send the reply back to the firewall — it replies directly to the backend over Layer 2.\n* The backend (`172.30.198.49`) receives a SYN-ACK from `172.30.198.33`, but it was expecting a response from `109.62.64.18`!\n* The backend kernel rejects the unexpected packet with a `RST`, and the connection dies.\n\n---\n\n### Step 4: The Result on Your Backend Terminal\nBecause the firewall drops the packet in Step 2:\n* No `SYN-ACK` ever returns.\n* After retransmissions, `curl` exits with:\n  ```text\n  curl: (28) Failed to connect to 109.62.64.18 port 9443: Connection timed out\n  ```\n\n---\n\n### Key Difference Between Port 443 and Port 9443:\n* **Port 443**: Fails because the firewall has **no VIP/DNAT rule at all** on `109.62.64.18:443`.\n* **Port 9443**: Has a valid DNAT rule on the firewall, but fails because the firewall **only applies it to incoming WAN/Internet traffic**, not internal LAN traffic (missing Hairpin NAT + SNAT).","thinking":"**Tracing the hairpin NAT.**\n\nThe previous query involved a non-existent DNAT rule for port 443; this query now considers port 9443 where a DNAT rule *does* exist. It strongly suggests Hairpin NAT is involved. It needs dissection of the packet lifecycle to accurately determine flow.\n\n\n**Analyzing firewall connection processing.**\n\nThe firewall receives the packet on the internal interface, and by default, DNAT rules are usually configured for the external interface. This means the DNAT rule isn't applied. Consequently, a firewall policy check is initiated to determine if a connection from the internal network to the external IP and port (109.62.64.18:9443) is allowed. If there is no such policy, the connection attempt gets dropped.\n\n\n"}
