{"step_index":307,"source":"MODEL","type":"PLANNER_RESPONSE","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-09-10T09:57:05Z","content":"To fix this and allow the backend (`172.30.198.49`) to access Map using **`https://109.62.64.18:9443`**, you have two approaches:\n\n---\n\n### Solution 1: Immediate Fix on the Backend (`172.30.198.49`)\n*(Takes 5 seconds, requires no changes to the corporate firewall)*\n\nOn Linux, the kernel can locally redirect outbound traffic intended for `109.62.64.18:9443` directly to the reverse proxy (`172.30.198.33:9443`) before it ever leaves the network card. \n\nThe application on `.49` believes it is talking to `109.62.64.18:9443`, and connection tracking handles the return packets seamlessly.\n\nRun this single command on `172.30.198.49`:\n```bash\nsudo iptables -t nat -A OUTPUT -p tcp -d 109.62.64.18 --dport 9443 -j DNAT --to-destination 172.30.198.33:9443\n```\n\n#### Why this works:\n1. When any service on `.49` calls `https://109.62.64.18:9443`, the kernel rewrites the destination to `172.30.198.33:9443`.\n2. The packet goes directly to the reverse proxy over the local network.\n3. The reverse proxy replies directly to `.49`.\n4. The kernel on `.49` un-translates the source back to `109.62.64.18`.\n5. **No firewall traversal, no hairpin drop, instant connectivity.**\n\n---\n\n### Solution 2: The Firewall Fix (Hairpin NAT / U-Turn NAT)\n*(If you have management access to the firewall at `172.30.198.54`)*\n\nTo solve this at the network level for all internal clients, you must configure a **Hairpin NAT policy** on your firewall:\n\n1. **Firewall Policy Rule**:\n   * **Incoming Interface**: Internal / LAN (VLAN `172.30.198.0/24`)\n   * **Outgoing Interface**: Internal / LAN (VLAN `172.30.198.0/24`)\n   * **Source**: `172.30.198.49` (or `172.30.198.0/24`)\n   * **Destination**: VIP `109.62.64.18` (mapped to `172.30.198.33:9443`)\n   * **Service / Port**: `TCP 9443`\n   * **Action**: `ACCEPT`\n2. **NAT Configuration**:\n   * **DNAT**: Translate destination `109.62.64.18` $\\rightarrow$ `172.30.198.33`.\n   * **SNAT (Source NAT / Masquerade)**: <span style=\"color:red\">**MANDATORY**</span>. \n     Translate the source IP to the firewall's internal IP (`172.30.198.54`). This forces the reverse proxy to send response packets back to the firewall rather than attempting a direct local Layer-2 reply, avoiding the asymmetric routing drop.\n\n---\n\n### Recommendation\n\nIf you want **Solution 1** (immediate fix on `172.30.198.49`), I can apply it right now and run a verification test with `curl https://109.62.64.18:9443/`. \n\nShall I apply this iptables rule on `172.30.198.49`?","thinking":"**Defining the Access Constraint**\n\nThe core requirement is successful access to \"Map\" via `https://109.62.64.18:9443`. This implies a hard constraint for external access, regardless of internal infrastructure. The solution needs to ensure requests to that specific IP and port combination always succeed.\n\n\n**Evaluating Network Connectivity Solutions**\n\nHairpin NAT on the firewall appears to be the most comprehensive solution for internal access to the external IP and port.  This approach requires carefully configuring SNAT and DNAT on the firewall to correctly handle traffic flow and return paths. SNAT implementation on the firewall is crucial to address potential routing problems that might arise with responses.\n\nAlternatively, a local rule on the backend host offers a potentially faster solution if firewall modification is restricted, but the feasibility depends on its configuration.  Considering both options provides flexibility, depending on the user's operational constraints and access.\n\n\n"}
