{"step_index":309,"source":"MODEL","type":"PLANNER_RESPONSE","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-09-10T10:00:26Z","content":"In `iptables-nft` (Debian/RHEL), the `tcp` module must be explicitly loaded with `-m tcp`.\n\nHere is the exact corrected command:\n\n```bash\niptables -t nat -A OUTPUT -p tcp -m tcp --dport 9443 -d 109.62.64.18 -j DNAT --to-destination 172.30.198.33:9443\n```\n\n---\n\n### Once applied, test immediately from the backend:\n```bash\ncurl -k -i https://109.62.64.18:9443/centreon-map/\n```\n\nIt should instantly return **`HTTP/2 401 Unauthorized`** from Centreon Map!\n\n---\n\n*(To make the rule persist across reboots once verified, run `apt-get install -y iptables-persistent` or `iptables-save > /etc/iptables/rules.v4`).*","thinking":"**Diagnosing iptables option error**\n\nThe error message \"unknown option '--dport'\" indicates an incorrect command syntax for the version of the tool being used. Further investigation suggests the need for the `-m tcp` module to be specified before `--dport`, or `-p tcp` requires `-m tcp --` to correctly define the destination port. This is a common requirement in this tool's usage, and the user likely needs to adjust the command accordingly.\n\n\n"}
